Because of historical reasons, the `runAsUser` and `runAsGroup` in `securityContext` is only designed for the Linux, not Windows. See [PodSpec](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/62aae86899a7b780eb1e8805fbcaf0fb6a5baa14/pkg/apis/core/types.go#L3394) for more details.

[KEP-2802](https://github.com/kubernetes/enhancements/tree/master/keps/sig-windows/2802-identify-windows-pods-apiserver-admission) adds a new field `podOS` to the PodSpec, which can be used to identify the OS of the pod.

```go
	// Specifies the OS of the containers in the pod.
	// Some pod and container fields are restricted if this is set.
	//
	// If the OS field is set to linux, the following fields must be unset:
	// - securityContext.windowsOptions
	//
	// If the OS field is set to windows, following fields must be unset:
	// - spec.hostPID
	// - spec.hostIPC
	// - spec.hostUsers
	// - spec.securityContext.appArmorProfile
	// - spec.securityContext.seLinuxOptions
	// - spec.securityContext.seccompProfile
	// - spec.securityContext.fsGroup
	// - spec.securityContext.fsGroupChangePolicy
	// - spec.securityContext.sysctls
	// - spec.shareProcessNamespace
	// - spec.securityContext.runAsUser
	// - spec.securityContext.runAsGroup
	// - spec.securityContext.supplementalGroups
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.appArmorProfile
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.seLinuxOptions
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.seccompProfile
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.capabilities
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.privileged
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.procMount
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.runAsUser
	// - spec.containers[*].securityContext.runAsGroup
	// +optional
	OS *PodOS
```

It's an optional field and no default value. If unset, the above fields are not restricted by the api-server. In other words, the following job is allowed to be created.

```go
job := &batchv1.Job{
	ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{
		Name:      jobName,
		Namespace: ns,
	},
	Spec: batchv1.JobSpec{
		BackoffLimit: ptr.To[int32](0),
		Template: v1.PodTemplateSpec{
			Spec: v1.PodSpec{
				RestartPolicy: v1.RestartPolicyNever,
				SecurityContext: &v1.PodSecurityContext{
					RunAsUser:    ptr.To[int64](999),        // windows does not support
					RunAsGroup:   ptr.To[int64](999),        // windows does not support
					RunAsNonRoot: ptr.To(true),
				},
				Tolerations: []v1.Toleration{
					{
						Key:    constants.LabelNodeRoleControlPlane,
						Effect: v1.TaintEffectNoSchedule,
					},
				},
				Containers: []v1.Container{
					{
						Name:  jobName,
						Image: images.GetPauseImage(cfg),
						Args:  []string{"-v"},
					},
				},
			},
		},
	},
}
```

But it will fails to start on Windows nodes. And the root cause is that the `os.Chown` is called by the [AtomicWriter](https://github.com/carlory/kubernetes/blob/7c48c2bd72b9bf5c44d21d7338cc7bea77d0ad2a/pkg/volume/util/atomic_writer.go#L447).

To fix this issue, the AtomicWriter can check the current OS of the node and skip the `os.Chown` if the OS is Windows. I want to confirm with sig-storage and sig-windows if this is the correct way to fix this issue.

I'm not sure if the failing job is expected or not. If it's expected, kubeadm has to change the job manifest to make it work on only Linux nodes. 

Why I ask this question is that I saw [volume.SetVolumeOwnership](https://github.com/carlory/kubernetes/blob/7c48c2bd72b9bf5c44d21d7338cc7bea77d0ad2a/pkg/volume/projected/projected.go#L238), which is used to set the ownership of the volume, if the os is Windows, it will become a no-op.


